// SEVERE — a signature-less module whose LAST STATEMENT IS AN
// EXPRESSION loses every export under fusion. The module's bindings
// never reach the variable channel, so every outside reference is dead:
//
//   interp: 41
//   jit:    CompileErr (in the oracle's module form) / silently NO
//           OUTPUT AT ALL (running the two-file form on the plain
//           binary — no error, no value, the program just never
//           produces)
//
// Trigger is exactly the trailing statement KIND, verified by
// substitution — with `let c = i64:41` as the module's last statement
// both modes print 41; with ANY trailing expression (`i64:0`, `c`,
// `c + i64:1`) the jit goes dead. Two-file form on the plain binary:
//
//   m9.gx:    let c = i64:41;
//             i64:0
//   main.gx:  mod m9;
//             m9::c
//   graphix --no-fusion main.gx  -> 41
//   graphix main.gx              -> (nothing)
//
// MECHANISM — a signature-less module is not a `Module` node at all.
// `ModuleKind::Resolved { sig: None }` (node/compiler.rs ~119-131)
// compiles it to `Block { module: true }`. Two independent consequences,
// both confirmed:
//
//  (1) DEAD-ELIMINATED. `stmt_subtree_effect_free`
//      (fusion/emit/flow.rs ~57-89) has an explicit
//      `NodeView::Module(_) => ok = false` arm whose comment says a
//      `mod` statement is never dead because it publishes its binds
//      into the persistent env. A sig-less module is a Block, so it
//      falls to the `_ => {}` arm, is classified effect-free, and
//      `emit_block_node`'s dead-statement elimination drops it whole.
//      Witness (two files):
//        m.gx:    let c = i64:5
//        main.gx: mod m;
//                 m::c <- m::c + i64:1;
//                 m::c
//      --no-fusion prints 5 6 7 8 ...; fusion prints NOTHING, ever, and
//      `--fusion-stats` reports `1 of 1 attempted regions fused,
//      0 blocked`.
//
//  (2) BLOCK-SCOPED. When the module statement DOES survive (it
//      contains a Connect, so it is not effect-free), it is emitted by
//      the ordinary do-block emitter, which takes `cx.env.mark()` at
//      entry and `truncate(mark)` at exit — so every binding the module
//      introduced is popped when the `mod` statement ends, and the
//      outer read falls back to the connect-target LIFT's entry param.
//      Witness (two files):
//        m.gx:    let c = i64:5;
//                 c <- c + i64:1
//        main.gx: mod m;
//                 m::c
//      --no-fusion prints 5 6 7 8 ...; fusion prints 6 7 8 9 ... — the
//      whole stream is shifted by one and the SEED value 5 is never
//      produced. A silent off-by-one in a module's exported stream.
//
// This program is the third face: no connect at all, so the module's
// binding just never reaches the variable channel.
//
// Fix: a `mod` statement must not be treated as an ordinary block.
//  - `stmt_subtree_effect_free` must key on "is a module statement",
//    not on `NodeView::Module` — a `Block { module: true }` is one too.
//  - The module's bindings must not be env-popped at the end of the
//    statement, and a bind referenced from OUTSIDE the region must
//    PUBLISH rather than stay an SSA local (the same escape analysis
//    that already treats a lifted connect target as external).
//  - Keeping the per-statement structure `Module::fuse` intends (~647,
//    "a module is structure, not computation") for the sig-less case
//    would achieve all of this at once.
//
// NOTE (corpus hygiene): this is a MULTI-FILE wrapper (`// file-v1:`
// sections, see graphix-fuzz/src/files.rs). In the oracle form the jit
// side is a COMPILE error, so the oracle reports the divergence; the
// two-file plain-binary form above is the one that shows the silent
// death, which is what a user would actually experience.
m9::c
// file-v1: m9.gx
let c = i64:41;
i64:0
